'''Old Uyghur''' ({{zh|t=回鶻語|回鶻語|s=回鹘语|回鹘语|p=Huíhú yǔ}}) was a [[Turkic languages|Turkic language]] spoken in [[Qocho]] from the 9th–14th centuries as well as in [[Gansu]].

==History==
[[File:Yuntai Uyghur east wall.jpg|thumb|Uyghur inscription on the east interior wall of the [[Cloud Platform at Juyong Pass]].]]

[[File:Yuntai Uyghur west wall.jpg|thumb|Uyghur inscription on the west interior wall of the [[Cloud Platform at Juyong Pass]].]]
Old Uyghur evolved from [[Old Turkic]], a [[Siberian Turkic languages|Siberian Turkic language]], after the [[Uyghur Khaganate]] broke up and remnants of it migrated to [[Turfan]], Qomul (later [[Hami]]), and [[Gansu]] in the ninth century.

The Uyghurs in Turfan and Qomul founded Qocho and adopted [[Manichaeism]] and [[Buddhism]] as their religions, while those in Gansu first founded the [[Ganzhou Uyghur Kingdom]] and became subjects of the [[Western Xia]]; their descendants are the [[Yugurs]] of Gansu. The [[Western Yugur language]] is the descendant of Old Uyghur.{{sfn|Clauson|1965|p=57}}

The Kingdom of Qocho survived as a client state of the [[Mongol Empire]] but was conquered by the Muslim [[Chagatai Khanate]], which conquered Turfan and Qomul and [[Islamized]] the region. Old Uyghur then became extinct in Turfan and Qomul.

The [[Uyghur language]] that is the official language of the [[Xinjiang Uyghur Autonomous Region]] is not descended from Old Uyghur. It is a descendant of the [[Karluk languages]] spoken in the [[Kara-Khanid Khanate]],<ref>Arik 2008, p. 145</ref> in particular the ''Khākānī'' language described by [[Mahmud al-Kashgari]]. The only surviving descendant of Old Uyghur is [[Western_Yugur_language|Yellow Yughur]], spoken in the [[Gansu]] region of China.

==Features==
Old Uyghur had an anticipating counting system, which is passed on to Western Yugur.<ref>Chen et al, 1985</ref>

==Literature==
{{See also|Bible translations into Uyghur|Maitrisimit nom bitig}}
Much of Old Uyghur literature is religious texts regarding [[Manichaeism]] and [[Buddhism]],<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://hk.plm.org.cn/e_book/xz-11348.pdf |title=西域、 敦煌文献所见回鹊之佛经翻译 |access-date=7 September 2015 |archive-date=19 May 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200519141042/http://hk.plm.org.cn/e_book/xz-11348.pdf/ |url-status=dead }}</ref> with examples found among the [[Dunhuang manuscripts]]. Multilingual inscriptions including Old Uyghur can be found at the [[Cloud Platform at Juyong Pass]] and the [[Stele of Sulaiman]].

== Script ==
{{Main|Old Uyghur alphabet}}

[[Qocho]], the [[History of the Uyghur people|Uyghur]] kingdom created in 843, originally used the "runic" [[Old Turkic alphabet]] with a "anïγ" dialect. The [[Old Uyghur alphabet]] was adopted from local inhabitants, along with a "ayïγ" dialect, when they migrated into Turfan after 840.<ref>{{citation |last = Sinor |first = D. |year = 1998 |title = History of Civilisations of Central Asia |editor1-last = Asimov |editor1-first = M.S. |editor2-last = Bosworth|editor2-first = C.E. |volume = 4 part II |chapter = Chapter 13 – Language situation and scripts |page = 333 |publisher = UNESCO Publishing |isbn = 81-208-1596-3 |chapter-url = https://unesdoc.unesco.org/ark:/48223/pf0000120455 }}</ref>

